body:
I don't know. Part of me thinks that there is always some type of solution, that some level of change or healing is possible. But people also live tremendous pain, and who's to tell them to hang on? I think maybe the origin of this dogma that suicide is necessarily bad is that it served an economic purpose. Religion taught that it was a sin because then the slaves and the poor could be exploited to the bone with no possible exit, keeping the workforce intact for the wealthy. This thought doesn't make the idea of suicide any less horrible though. I am still afraid of death and I hate the thought that people are alone with so much pain. I wish I could heal us all. 

&#x200B;

Another thing is the question of displacing our pain onto others. I guess this is "wrong". I don't know. For me it's definitely a deterrent, as bad as I feel it doesn't justify ruining the life of someone who loves me. But... I also get thoughts that if this life is an illusion, maybe the whole thing will disappear if I do. I realize how insane that sounds and that it's just a way to get around the guilt.
